    summary: 'This case elaborated on the rarest of rare doctrine established in Bachan Singh v. State of Punjab (1980). The Supreme Court laid down five categories where the death penalty may be warranted: (1) manner of commission is extremely brutal, (2) motive reveals total depravity, (3) anti-social or socially abhorrent nature, (4) magnitude of crime affecting multiple victims, and (5) personality of victim (child, helpless woman, elderly). These categories became the standard framework for death penalty jurisprudence.',
