    keyLaws: ['Section 302 of IPC', 'Section 149 IPC', 'Section 148 IPC', 'Section 354(3) CrPC'],
    fullText: 'Macchi Singh v. State of Punjab is a seminal case that operationalized the rarest of rare doctrine laid down in Bachan Singh v. State of Punjab (1980). The case arose from a brutal massacre in which Macchi Singh and his associates killed 17 members of a rival faction across three villages in Punjab on the night of January 24-25, 1977, as an act of revenge. The trial court convicted the accused and sentenced several to death. On appeal, the question before the Supreme Court was whether the case fell within the rarest of rare category. Justice M.P. Thakkar, writing for the bench, laid down a comprehensive framework of five categories to guide courts in determining whether a case qualifies as rarest of rare: (1) Manner of commission — when the murder is committed in an extremely brutal, grotesque, or diabolical manner to arouse intense indignation; (2) Motive — when the murder is committed for a motive displaying total depravity and meanness; (3) Anti-social or socially abhorrent nature — such as dowry deaths or bride burning; (4) Magnitude — when the crime is enormous in proportion, such as multiple murders of a family or mass murders; (5) Personality of victim — when the victim is a helpless child, woman, elderly person, or public figure. The court held that the balancing test requires weighing aggravating circumstances against mitigating ones, and death penalty is warranted only when the option of life imprisonment is unquestionably foreclosed.',
