    fullText: 'Shatrughan Chauhan v. Union of India is a landmark judgment on the rights of death row prisoners. The case consolidated petitions from 15 death row convicts whose mercy petitions had been pending before the President for years—in some cases over a decade. The petitioners argued that the long delay in deciding mercy petitions, combined with the suffering of death row, constituted cruel and inhuman treatment violating Article 21. The Supreme Court bench of Justices P. Sathasivam, Ranjan Gogoi, and Shiva Kirti Singh accepted this argument and commuted all 15 death sentences to life imprisonment. The court held that inexplicable delay in execution of death sentence by itself is sufficient to entitle the prisoner to commutation. The court identified several supervening circumstances that warrant commutation: (1) undue delay in execution — where there has been a long delay in disposing of the mercy petition; (2) insanity or mental illness of convict; (3) solitary confinement on death row, which the court held to be unconstitutional. The court directed that death row prisoners should not be kept in solitary confinement prior to rejection of mercy petition and that prisoners must be informed of the rejection of their mercy petition and given 14 days before execution. This case strengthened the procedural safeguards for death row prisoners.',
