    fullText: 'Lalita Kumari v. Government of Uttar Pradesh addressed the long-standing controversy over whether police are duty-bound to register an FIR upon receiving information of a cognizable offense. The case arose when Lalita Kumari\'s minor daughter was kidnapped and the UP police refused to register an FIR despite receiving a complaint. A habeas corpus petition was filed, and the matter eventually reached a five-judge Constitution bench. The court examined whether FIR registration under Section 154 CrPC is mandatory or involves police discretion. The bench unanimously held that registration of FIR is mandatory under Section 154 of the CrPC if the information discloses commission of a cognizable offense. The word "shall" in Section 154 makes it imperative. The police officer cannot conduct a preliminary inquiry to determine whether to register the FIR. If a cognizable offense is made out, the FIR must be registered. However, the court carved exceptions for certain categories: matrimonial/family disputes, commercial offenses, medical negligence, corruption against public servants, and cases involving statutory violations (like food adulteration). In these categories, a preliminary inquiry of up to 7 days is permitted. The court directed that all district and state police headquarters must display FIR registration information and that failure to register an FIR shall attract departmental action.',
