    fullText: 'Selvi v. State of Karnataka is a landmark judgment on the intersection of neuroscience and constitutional rights. The case arose from several appeals challenging the involuntary administration of narco analysis tests, brain electrical activation profile (BEAP/brain mapping) tests, and polygraph (lie detector) tests on accused persons and witnesses by investigating agencies. A three-judge bench examined whether these techniques violate the rights guaranteed under Articles 20(3) and 21 of the Constitution. The court conducted an extensive analysis of the scientific basis and reliability of these tests. Regarding Article 20(3) (right against self-incrimination), the court held that compelling a person to undergo these tests amounts to testimonial compulsion, as these tests are designed to extract information from the subject\'s mind. The protection under Article 20(3) extends not only to oral or written statements but also to any form of communication, including physiological responses elicited during these tests. The court further held that the involuntary administration of these tests violates the right to privacy and personal liberty under Article 21. The court ruled that these tests can only be administered with the informed consent of the subject, meaning the subject must be informed of the purpose, procedure, and their right to refuse. Results of voluntarily taken tests are admissible only as corroborative evidence under Section 27 of the Indian Evidence Act, not as substantive evidence.',
