    fullText: 'Bilkis Yakub Rasool v. Union of India addressed the controversial premature release of 11 convicts in the Bilkis Bano gang rape case. During the 2002 Gujarat communal riots, Bilkis Bano, a 19-year-old pregnant Muslim woman, was gang-raped and 14 members of her family, including her three-year-old daughter, were murdered by a mob. The case was transferred to a Mumbai court on Supreme Court orders due to concerns about fair trial in Gujarat. In 2008, a Mumbai special court convicted 11 accused and sentenced them to life imprisonment. On August 15, 2022, the Gujarat government released all 11 convicts under its remission policy, sparking nationwide outrage. The convicts were garlanded upon release. Bilkis Bano challenged the remission orders before the Supreme Court. A bench of Justices B.V. Nagarathna and Ujjal Bhuyan in January 2024 quashed the premature release orders on multiple grounds: the Gujarat government had no jurisdiction to grant remission as the trial was conducted in Maharashtra on Supreme Court orders, the remission was granted without consulting the presiding judge of the trial court as required, and the victims and the CBI (prosecution) were not heard before granting remission. The court directed all 11 convicts to surrender within two weeks. The judgment reaffirmed that remission is not a right and victims have a right to be heard in remission proceedings.',
