    keyLaws: ['Prevention of Corruption Act 1988', 'Section 197 CrPC', 'Section 19 Prevention of Corruption Act'],
    fullText: 'K. Anbazhagan v. Superintendent of Police addressed the interplay between the requirement of prior sanction for prosecution of public servants and the fight against corruption. The case involved prosecution of public servants under the Prevention of Corruption Act 1988. The question was whether sanction under Section 197 CrPC was necessary before initiating prosecution. The Supreme Court held that the protection of sanction under Section 197 CrPC is available only when the alleged offense has a reasonable connection with the discharge of official duty. If the criminal act has no nexus with the official duty, no sanction is required. The court distinguished between acts done in the course of official duty and acts that merely take advantage of the official position. Corruption and bribery cannot be said to be part of an official\'s duties. The court further clarified the scope of Section 19 of the Prevention of Corruption Act, which requires sanction before a court can take cognizance of an offense under the Act. The court held that this sanction must be obtained in a timely manner and cannot be used as a shield to delay prosecution indefinitely. The decision strengthened the prosecution framework against corrupt officials.',
