    fullText: 'Sanjay Chandra v. CBI is a leading authority on bail jurisprudence in India, particularly in white-collar crime cases. The case arose from the 2G spectrum allocation scam, one of the largest corruption scandals in India. Sanjay Chandra, managing director of Unitech, and other corporate executives were arrested for their alleged role in obtaining 2G spectrum licenses through fraudulent means, causing an estimated loss of Rs 1.76 lakh crore to the exchequer. They had been refused bail by the trial court and the High Court. The Supreme Court, in a judgment by Justice Aftab Alam and Justice R.M. Lodha, granted bail, reiterating the settled principle that the basic rule is bail, not jail. The court held that the gravity of the offense alone cannot be a ground to deny bail, especially when the accused are not likely to flee, tamper with evidence, or influence witnesses. The court emphasized that pretrial detention should not be punitive in nature. When the trial is likely to take considerable time and the accused has already been incarcerated for several months, continued detention amounts to punishment before conviction, violating Article 21. The court noted that all the accused had deep roots in society, were cooperating with the investigation, and there was no evidence of flight risk. This judgment has been extensively cited in subsequent bail applications involving economic offenses.',
