    fullText: 'J. Jayalalithaa v. State of Karnataka is a landmark corruption case involving disproportionate assets of a sitting Chief Minister. The case was filed in 1997 by DMK leader K. Anbazhagan alleging that Jayalalithaa, during her tenure as Chief Minister of Tamil Nadu (1991-1996), amassed wealth disproportionate to her known sources of income. The total disproportionate assets were estimated at Rs 66.65 crore. The case was transferred to Karnataka on Supreme Court orders to ensure impartial trial. The Special Court in Bangalore convicted Jayalalithaa and co-accused V.K. Sasikala, Ilavarasi, and V.N. Sudhakaran under Section 13(1)(e) of the Prevention of Corruption Act. The Karnataka High Court in 2015 acquitted all accused, finding that the prosecution had not established the charges beyond reasonable doubt. On appeal, the Supreme Court in February 2017 reversed the High Court acquittal and restored the trial court conviction. The court meticulously analyzed the evidence of 32 properties acquired during the relevant period, financial transactions, and benami holdings. The court held that the wealth was manifestly disproportionate and the accused had failed to satisfactorily account for it. Since Jayalalithaa had died on December 5, 2016, the proceedings against her abated. The co-accused were sentenced to 4 years imprisonment and fined Rs 10 crore each.',
